@* The creation steps, in the order the CLI wizard runs them. Every step stays
   reachable at any time — the terminal wizard has back-navigation for the same reason,
   and a browser that only moved forwards would be a step backwards from it.

   **The list itself is not here.** It is beside the command palette's other commands, because
   the palette offers these same six and two lists would drift: a palette that offered a step
   this band does not have, or missed one it does, is worse than no palette, and nothing about
   the two living in different files would have caught it. This band draws whatever that list
   holds, so they cannot disagree. *@

@* `.steps` is on the <nav>, which is the band, and the list inside it is the centred column.
   The class stayed on the outer element on purpose: the print block hides `.steps`, and moving
   it to the list would leave the band's own padding and bottom rule printing around nothing.

   The band carries that rule because the budget strip below it is absent on three whole classes
   of screen — Villain mode, the tier page before a tier is chosen, and every replay route — so
   nothing else closes the chrome there. See the comment on `.steps` in app.css. *@
<nav class="steps" aria-label="Creation steps">
    <ul class="steps-list">
        @for (var i = 0; i < Commands.Steps.Count; i++)
        {
            var step = Commands.Steps[i];
            var index = i + 1;
            <li>
                <NavLink class="" href="@step.Target" Match="NavLinkMatch.All" ActiveClass="active">
                    <span class="step-index">@index</span>@step.Label
                </NavLink>
            </li>
        }
    </ul>

    @* A link off the builder's own chrome rather than a step of its own — Resolve and Adversity
       are not something anybody selects while creating a character, but a reader mid-build who
       wants to check what a Defining Moment costs should not have to leave the tool to find out.
       See docs/guide/browser.md's narrow exemption for why this page can read resolve.json at
       all, and EveryRoutedPageIsReachableFromAnotherPage for why it needs a link at all. *@
    <a class="small" href="reference/resolve">Resolve &amp; Adversity reference</a>
</nav>
